{
  "assembly_notes": "Plumbing/tubing flaring tool.  A split clamp bar (root) carries N progressively sized tube seats plus the continuous flare groove that links them.  A cast yoke BRIDGES the working seat in X -- its legs come down fore and aft of the seat and seat on the bar's top face with a 0.6 mm captured lap -- and hosts the rotary feed screw (revolute on the true spindle Z axis, registered via mate_axes/register_interface_mate) and the flaring cone (prismatic -Z on the same axis, constrained_by the feed screw).  The yoke deliberately does not straddle the bar in Y: at the yoke station the hinged moving jaw sweeps a*tan(q) sideways, so a Y cheek is struck long before the clamp is usefully open; bridging in X keeps the whole yoke above the bar's top plane on the jaw side and makes the clamp-open and yoke poses independent.  A side yoke-lock screw turns about Y, captured by two ball detents inside its real bore (a shoulder bearing on the boss face inflates on world AABBs when the yoke pivots).  handle_style, yoke_frame, yoke_mount and end_clamp are independent structural slots; every combination builds through the shared derivations (yoke bore > cone envelope, collar bore > screw shaft, knuckle bore > pin, lock/clamp bore > stem, groove half width > cone radius at the bar's top plane), so no whole-part or per-element overlap allowance is used anywhere.  tube_hole_count is a multiplicity on end_clamp.",
